--TEST--
`Core\Response::addCookie` enforces the `__Host-` and `__Secure-` prefixes rather than documenting them, so a non-conforming cookie is refused on write -- `rule:errors/cookie-name-bytes`, spec § 15
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Agreement, asked of the two prefixes: what makes each cookie legal is the
// *name* it carries and nothing about the options beside it. A prefix whose
// meaning depends on every call site remembering it is the arrangement that
// produced CVE-2024-2756, so the rule is asked here once.

// `__Host-` requires `Secure`, requires `Path=/` and forbids `Domain` — and a
// bare call conforms, because ADR 0074 § 3's defaults are exactly those two.
// That is the whole of how a program writes a host-locked cookie: by naming it.
uint $accepted = 0;
try {
    Core\Response::addCookie("__Host-sid", "abc");
    $accepted = $accepted + 1;
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
}
echo "a bare __Host- cookie conformed ", $accepted, " time\n";

// Each of the three conditions, broken one at a time. Every one of them is a
// cookie a browser would refuse to store, so writing it and learning nothing
// is the failure this refusal removes.
uint $host = 0;
try {
    Core\Response::addCookie("__Host-sid", "abc", {secure: false});
} catch (LogicError $insecure) {
    $host = $host + 1;
}
try {
    Core\Response::addCookie("__Host-sid", "abc", {path: "/app"});
} catch (LogicError $narrowed) {
    $host = $host + 1;
}
try {
    Core\Response::addCookie("__Host-sid", "abc", {domain: "example.com"});
} catch (LogicError $shared) {
    $host = $host + 1;
}
echo "__Host- refused ", $host, " of 3\n";

// `__Secure-` is the weaker of the two and asks for one thing only, which is
// what makes this an agreement and not a repeat: the same member reads a
// different rule off a different prefix, so a path and a domain that `__Host-`
// refuses are admitted here.
uint $secure = 0;
try {
    Core\Response::addCookie("__Secure-sid", "abc");
    $secure = $secure + 1;
} catch (LogicError $bare) {
}
try {
    Core\Response::addCookie("__Secure-sid", "abc", {path: "/app", domain: "example.com"});
    $secure = $secure + 1;
} catch (LogicError $scoped) {
}
echo "__Secure- admitted ", $secure, " of 2\n";

uint $refusedSecure = 0;
try {
    Core\Response::addCookie("__Secure-sid", "abc", {secure: false});
} catch (LogicError $plain) {
    $refusedSecure = $refusedSecure + 1;
}
echo "__Secure- refused ", $refusedSecure, " of 1\n";

// And a name carrying neither prefix is bound by neither rule: the same three
// options that broke `__Host-` above are an ordinary cookie here. The prefix is
// the whole of what is being read.
uint $plainCookie = 0;
try {
    Core\Response::addCookie("sid", "abc", {secure: false, path: "/app", domain: "example.com"});
    $plainCookie = $plainCookie + 1;
} catch (LogicError $ordinary) {
}
echo "an unprefixed cookie took all three ", $plainCookie, " time\n";

// The sentence says which prefix and what it requires, so the program learns
// the rule at the moment it broke it.
try {
    Core\Response::addCookie("__Host-sid", "abc", {domain: "example.com"});
} catch (LogicError $why) {
    echo $why->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
a bare __Host- cookie conformed 1 time
__Host- refused 3 of 3
__Secure- admitted 2 of 2
__Secure- refused 1 of 1
an unprefixed cookie took all three 1 time
Core\Response::addCookie(): `__Host-sid` carries the `__Host-` prefix, which the runtime enforces rather than documents: it requires `secure: true`, requires `path: "/"` and forbids `domain`. A cookie that does not conform is not visible on read, so it is refused on write
